+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; print(Path(\\\"/Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/autopilot/0.10.0/skills/run-autopilot/SKILL.md\\\").read_text()[14500:20500]); print(Path(\\\"docs/dev/project-management/meta/project-capsule.md\\\").read_text()[:5500]); print(Path(\\\"/Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/autopilot/0.10.0/skills/plan-tasks/SKILL.md\\\").read_text()[14500:18500])'\"","aggregated_output":"tmp/`, the only tree `purge-devtmp` touches.\n- **Disposable** (removed by their lifecycle transitions, by name): `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-session.log`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wrapper.log`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/pause-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/paused-by-operator`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/session-brief.md` (re-rendered at every handoff), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.session-left` (overwritten at every loop-mode handoff), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/lanes/` (one marker per live CLI reviewer lane, removed when the lane exits), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` (at batch end only — in loop mode the wrapper archives it to `reports/{batch_id}-state-final.json`; see `references/phase-done.md`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wave.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/wave-slots/` and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/review-paths` (a wave's control file, its per-wave review-slot directory, and the review's path-scoping list — the last removed with the assembly worktree at wave end, same lifecycle as the other two — none is a durable PRD-lifecycle artifact; `references/waves.md`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/replan-context.md`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json.bak`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/**/*.lock` (the flock files beside `state.json` and beside each `deferred/*.json`), `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.turn-counts.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.handoff-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.cap-fired`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/contract-card.md`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/park-requested`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-verification.json`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.review-gate-blocks`, `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.review-gate-failed`, and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/.lane-guard-blocks`.\n\nThe machine form of this list is `docs/dev/project-management/.gitignore`, shipped by the Phase 0 `mkdir` step; a test keeps the two in step.\n\nTemporary review context and other reproducible scratch material belongs under the globally ignored `docs/dev/tmp/` tree. `purge-devtmp` operates only there, preserves files linked to live PRDs, and never traverses `docs/dev/project-management/`.\n\nBatch end and cleanup steps enumerate lifecycle-owned runtime files explicitly; they never remove a directory wholesale. This keeps a completed PRD's complete review trail intact after the batch closes (`references/design-rationale.md` § Verified moves).\n\n### Resuming\n\nWhen `/autopilot:run-autopilot` is invoked and `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` exists with `batch.completed_prds`, this is a continuation after a session restart. Preserve `batch.completed_prds` (including `batch.id`) and proceed to the build gate's Phase 0 to pick the next PRD.\n\nDelete `state.pause_reason` from `state.json` if present — unconditional, on every invocation, NOT gated on `batch.completed_prds`. A new session means any pause is being resumed; `pause_reason` belongs only to an unresolved pause and is not overwritten by normal progression, so it must be cleared here or it halts the resumed PRD's next hand-off. Cap-pause detection is unaffected — it keys on `cap_pause_reason`, which this delete does not touch.\n\n### Operator runbook (unattended batches)\n\nAll interaction with a running `autoclaude` batch happens at session boundaries — the only safe point. (Operator-shell commands for the human's own terminal, not for the model's Bash tool — `tail -f` would hang a session and pipes are hook-blocked.)\n\n- **Watch**: `tail -f docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/last-session.log` — the wrapper tees the live event log of the running headless turn there.\n- **Pause**: `touch docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/pause-requested` — the wrapper consumes the marker at the next session boundary, notifies \"paused by operator\", and stops the loop with state intact. It leaves `paused-by-operator` behind so tracon shows `⏸ paused` rather than `⚠ orphaned`; plain `autoclaude` resumes (nothing to clear, no decision to make). The marker may carry a JSON reason, `{\"reason\": \"...\"}`, which the stop line and the notification quote; a session that stands down for a peer writes one with a `condition` beside it, after asking the peer first (§ Session Loop, stand-down procedure).\n- **Take over**: with the loop stopped (pause marker, or Ctrl-C on the wrapper), run `/autopilot:run-autopilot` in a normal interactive session — resume-by-artifact reads the same `state.json`, and full interactive semantics (questions, PAUSEs) apply because `$_AUTOPILOT_LOOP` is unset. Restart `autoclaude` afterwards to go unattended again.\n- **Resume after a PAUSE** (`phase: \"paused\"` in `state.json`, \"session paused\" banner/ntfy): same take-over recipe, and it is the ONLY way forward — re-running `autoclaude` reads the paused state and exits again immediately. Interactively, the phase that paused re-runs with `AskUserQuestion` available, so the blockers that halted the loop are asked as decisions instead. Answer them, let the session hand off or finish the PRD, then re-run `autoclaude`.\n- **Un-park a sidelined PRD**: a PRD the loop parked (a `wrapper_died` death-park, or a loop-mode `design_gate` / `blocking_escalation` stall) sits in `docs/dev/project-management/prds/hold/` with its stall recorded in the batch deferred JSON; the batch kept draining without it. To resume it, `mv docs/dev/project-management/prds/hold/<00XXX-…>.md docs/dev/project-management/prds/backlog/` (keep the `00XXX-` prefix). Autopilot never reads `hold/`, so the next `/autopilot:run-autopilot` (or `autoclaude` relaunch) re-selects it from `backlog/` and re-enters the build gate, reusing its existing design doc via the Phase 1.5 artifact skip (a `design_gate` park then re-fires the gate interactively). Batch\n# Project Capsule: buvis-gems\n\nGenerated: 2026-10-29\n\n## Key Invariants\n- Commands return `CommandResult`; only `cli.py`/adapters render via `console`. No print/click.echo/logging.\n- Persist with `pybase.filesystem.atomic_write`, never bare `write_text`.\n- dot: one action, one implementation across CLI/TUI (`DotGitService` is the seam, PRD 00053). TUI must call the same command classes as CLI.\n- Lazy imports inside Click handlers.\n- Every CLI change updates `docs/source/tools/<tool>.rst` and `README.md`.\n- sysup: a string step or refresh command runs as `bash -eo pipefail -c <line>`; an argv list runs without a shell; either may be `{argv, env}` with `env` merged over the parent for that step only. Two expansion layers: the loader expands `${VAR}` first, bash second; `$${...}` passes through to bash. Sudo priming triggers on `sudo` as a word anywhere in the line, not only argv[0].\n- sysup spawn seam is `_run` in `runner.py` (tests mock `sysup.runner._run`, never `subprocess.run`). Only a captured step with a timeout runs in its own process group: the timeout SIGKILLs the group and Ctrl-C forwards SIGINT to it, because a background group never sees the terminal's SIGINT and cannot read the tty (a prompt stops it until the timeout). Everything else stays in the terminal's group: interactive steps must read the tty for sudo and brew prompts, and a step without a timeout has nothing to kill. Residuals (#252): an interactive step's timeout kills bash alone, and a command running as root through sudo cannot be signaled by sysup at all (`sudo -n kill -- -<pgid>` off the primed ticket is the upgrade path, `ponytail:` note in `_run`). `start_new_session` is wrong here: it drops the controlling tty and sudo's tty-keyed ticket. No bundled entry sets `timeout`.\n- Never `git init`/`git clone` inside the checkout under the Seatbelt profile: `.git/hooks/` creation is denied, the half-made repo falls through to gems' own `.git`, and commits land in gems as `Test User` (dot's fixture identity). `dev/local/` is gitignored since d314a3bf for the same incident.\n\n## Architecture Decisions\n- Single PyPI package `buvis-gems`; tools are top-level packages in the wheel.\n- dot works against a bare dotfiles repo (`cfg` wrapper, work-tree `$HOME`); `dotfiles_root` is resolved in `cli.py` (`DOTFILES_ROOT` env).\n- sysup steps are shell lines (v0.16.0, PR #253). This reverses decision #2 of PRD 00079 (argv lists, no shell): the user config had already grown `sh -ec` script steps, so \"no shell\" had only moved the shell into the data. `-e` stops a multi-command step at its first failure; `pipefail` fails the step on any failing pipe member.\n- The bundled `brew` step runs interactively with `--no-ask` and `HOMEBREW_NO_UPGRADE_AUTO_UPDATES_CASKS=1`: interactive so cask sudo prompts are visible (0.15.2), `--no-ask` so the proceed prompt cannot stall the run (0.15.3), the env var so self-updating casks are left alone (0.16.0). Three patch releases in two days came from that one step; change it with all three constraints in mind.\n- postup ships its committed SvelteKit build; the hatch hook rebuilds it only for release wheels (41bcbcbb). A Renovate bump of the postup frontend lock merges green with stale assets until someone runs `npm ci` + `npm run build` and commits `chore(postup): rebuild web frontend assets`.\n\n## Component Boundaries\n- `src/tools/dot/`: `cli.py` -> `commands/<name>/` -> `git/service.py` (`DotGitService`); TUI (`tui/app.py`, `tui/screens/main.py`, `tui/widgets/status_bar.py`) drives the same commands.\n- dot's host-filter surface lives in `src/tools/dot/git/filter_driver.py` as `FilterDriverMixin` (split out of `service.py` at 861 lines, over the 800 max); `_literal` moved there and `service.py` imports it from there.\n- sysup capabilities yield `StepResult`; config validates their `inputs` against `with:` and the runner owns dry-run, error conversion, command stamping, and run-log markers. Registering a capability therefore exposes it through the existing shared runner without another interface implementation.\n- sysup step shape normalization (string/list/object -> argv + `label`) is a config-layer concern (`StepSpec`); `runner.py` only sees resolved argv, the child env and the label used for dry-run and the run log. The timeout ceiling above lives in the runner because that is where the spawn seam is.\n\n## Active Work\n- No active autopilot batch: `autopilot/state.json` is absent, `prds/wip/` and `prds/backlog/` do not exist, `hold/` is empty. Last batch 202610261200 (00108) drained; its one deferred VERIFY is resolved (210e674c) and its test follow-ups closed 2026-10-29: `test_moves_every_well_formed_receipt_in_place` covers every receipt shape, pinned or not, including the two `future = false` receipts 00105 used to skip.\n- Last shipped work: sysup shell-line steps (PR #253, merged 2026-10-28), released as gems-v0.16.0 on 2026-10-29 (tag pushed, publish run in flight at catchup time).\n- #252 (timeout orphaning from that PR's review) fixed 2026-10-29 for captured steps; the interactive residual is recorded in the invariants.\n- Backup intake 00107 was specced on 2026-10-29 into five sequenced backlog PRDs: 00107 per-instance excludes, 00109 bundle `items` + skip outcomes + replace-after-complete, 00110 `--plan` + once-per-run output resolution, 00111 `--jobs` + cancellation, 00112 progress + `--quiet`. Two discovery answers were reversed at that point (no no-clobber or `--force`; a missing source root skips rather tha\neparable two-file task may become two independently gated one-file tasks. Count only expected implementor writes: Tess-owned tests that Ivan must only read are outside that write set, while tests Ivan must edit count as writes.\n\nWhen **both** triggers apply to the same task, a **single split pass** satisfies both — do not run two passes. After splitting, each subtask is re-estimated per step 4.5 and re-classified per step 4.7 (so `qwen_eligible` reflects the new file count).\n\n**Qwen infra preflight (gates the eligibility trigger only):**\n\nBefore applying the eligibility split trigger, `plan-tasks` runs the qwen infra preflight defined in `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/work/references/qwen-integration.md` (PRD 00031) — `pi` on PATH, the llama.cpp endpoint reachable, the configured model id present, and a real 1-token completion succeeding (the only check that proves the inference worker actually spawns). It is cheap on the common path; it exists so the eligibility split is only paid for when qwen can actually consume the result — a backend that lists the model but can't serve a token (`completion_failed`) skips the split rather than marking tasks qwen-eligible that would only fall back at dispatch.\n\n- **Healthy** → the eligibility trigger is active for this PRD's tasks.\n- **Unhealthy, or the probe is unavailable** (any failure mode reported by the probe — see `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/work/references/qwen-integration.md` for the current check list; absence of that file itself, e.g. before PRD 00031 has landed, also counts as unavailable) → the eligibility trigger is **skipped entirely**; tasks keep their original shape and route to Claude.\n\nThe **context-budget trigger is not gated** by the preflight and remains active regardless of qwen's status. The **step-4.7 `qwen_eligible` computation is not gated** either — it is always computed and persisted on every task (staying inert until `work` reads it).\n\n**Risk exemption (skips the eligibility trigger only):**\n\nA task carrying either risk fact (`contract_edit` or `algorithmic_risk`) is **not split for eligibility**. A risk fact normally lands the task on `opus`, which is never qwen-eligible, so the split would buy nothing. The exemption holds either way, and does not depend on that tier landing: a test-only or packaging-only slice pins `sonnet` before either fact is read. Judge both facts under step 4.7's evidence rules before splitting, and reuse the same values there. The exemption short-circuits the eligibility trigger and the task keeps its original shape. The context-budget trigger is unaffected: a risky task that also exceeds the context budget is still split for that reason.\n\nThe existing context-budget split mechanics, the one-split-attempt rule, and the stall behavior below are **unchanged** by the eligibility trigger — both triggers share them:\n\n1. **File boundary first.** Split into one task per file. The PRD slice prorates equally; the 55K overhead applies once per task. Re-estimate each subtask.\n2. **Capability boundary second.** If a task touches only one file and still exceeds the threshold, split along capability boundaries inside the PRD's Functional Decomposition section (or the decomposition derived from the Requirements in step 3 when the PRD states none).\n3. **One split attempt only.** If a task still exceeds the threshold after splitting, mark the PRD as stalled (use the threshold value in the stall_reason).\n\n**Stall behavior:**\n\nWhen unable to split below the threshold (150K standard, 75K in replan mode), **merge** a `stall_reason` key into the existing `docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json` via statectl — the sole writer for state.json (never hand-edit with Read/Write/Edit; see `/autopilot:run-autopilot`):\n\n```bash\npython3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/run-autopilot/scripts/statectl.py docs/dev/project-management/autopilot/state.json set stall_reason '{\"stalled\": \"oversized_task\", \"task\": \"<task-id>\", \"estimated_tokens\": <int>}'\n```\n\nstatectl \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
