+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c \\\"from pathlib import Path; print(Path('/Users/bob/.claude/plugins/cache/buvis-plugins/autopilot/0.10.0/skills/work/references/test-author-prompt.md').read_text()); print(Path('docs/dev/project-management/prds/wip/00111-backup-parallel-jobs-and-cancellation-v1.md').read_text())\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"# Test Author Prompt (Tess)\n\nTess writes tests from requirements. She has NOT seen and must NOT think about implementation.\n\n## Prompt Template\n\nThe prompt template lives in `work/references/tess-prompt.md` (single source\nof truth, registry-shaped single-brace placeholders — `render_prompt.py`\nconsumes it directly; see `agent-registry.md` for the placeholder table).\n\n## Context Selection\n\nWhen building Tess's prompt, include:\n\n| Include | Why |\n|---------|-----|\n| Task description + acceptance criteria | The spec Tess tests against |\n| Public types/interfaces | So Tess knows the API surface |\n| One sample test file | So Tess follows project conventions |\n| Test framework config | So imports and assertions are correct |\n| `<dir>/tests/HARNESS_CONTRACT.md`, when it exists | The project's own harness rules, for each Contract path `<dir>/<file>` the task touches |\n\n| Exclude | Why |\n|---------|-----|\n| Architecture docs | Would leak implementation thinking |\n| AGENTS.md internals | Same - Tess doesn't need to know how things are built |\n| Implementation files | Defeats the entire purpose |\n| \"How to build this\" guidance | Tess is a test author, not an implementor |\n\nMoved verbatim out of `SKILL.md` step 2.7 (PRD 00119-v2) — the two lists the\nstep used to spell out inline. **Read this section before the first Tess\ndispatch of a batch.**\n\n**Tess receives:**\n- Task description and acceptance criteria\n- The **exact file paths** the task touches — spelled **absolute** — and the **exact symbol names** to test, taken from the plan task — not \"find the relevant file\"\n- Public interfaces/types relevant to the task\n- The project's test-harness contract when one exists (PRD 00141): for each Contract path `<dir>/<file>`, `<dir>/tests/HARNESS_CONTRACT.md` joins the `PUBLIC_INTERFACES` `--set-cmd`, once per distinct file; no such file adds nothing. It carries harness rules, never implementation — a harness can make a test pass for reasons requirements cannot reveal (a `capture_main` that installs its own `sys.stdin`, so a \"marker checked before stdin\" test passes against the old code too)\n- Existing test patterns (one sample test file from the project)\n- Test framework and conventions used\n\n**Tess does NOT receive:**\n- Implementation strategy or architecture docs (loaded in step 2.5 for the main session and Ivan only)\n- \"How to build this\" context\n- Access to modify non-test files\n\nThe stdout integer from step 2.7's render call **is** the Subagent Dispatch Budget measurement — no separate `wc -c`. `tess-prompt.md` bakes in the read-only-scope instruction, the dispatch prologue, the Assumptions footer and the size limits (rule 12: functions under 50 lines, files under 800) permanently (mirroring `ivan.md`), so nothing further needs adding to the prompt by hand — open-ended discovery is where subagents burn turns and stall, and keeping Tess scoped to the listed files/symbols is the template's job now. Dispatch the Agent tool with the file at `docs/dev/tmp/dispatch-tess-<task-id>.txt` as the prompt source. The template also embeds Simplicity/Think-Before-Coding/Surgical rules to prevent Tess from writing speculative tests or silently assuming input shape.\n\n## Quality gate (step 2.8)\n\nMoved verbatim out of `SKILL.md` step 2.8 (PRD 00119-v2). SKILL.md keeps the\ngate rule and the total-Tess budget; the rubric and the retry render live here.\n**Read this section before running the gate on Tess's first test file.**\n\n**Run the computed shape check first**, on every test file Tess wrote or changed:\n\n```bash\npython3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/review-work-completion/scripts/detect_tautological_tests.py <test file>...\n```\n\nIt prints one `[MECH] {emoji} <test> <reason> | File: <path>:<line> | Task: general` line per test whose shape cannot fail (a constant or self-comparing assert, an `A or B` hedge, a swallowed exception, `raises(Exception)`, no assertion at all) and always exits 0. **Every `[MECH]` line is a check-4 failure**: copy it verbatim into the `QUALITY_FEEDBACK` file, then judge the four checks below by reading. The script sees shape only; a test that restates the code in a perfectly valid shape is still check 4's to catch by hand. The same script runs at review (`review-work-completion` step 3), so a shape that slips past here comes back as a `mech-check` finding and a rework cycle — cheaper to fix now.\n\nBefore committing Tess's tests, review them in the main session against this checklist:\n\n1. **Behavior names?** Each test name describes a behavior (\"rejects empty email\"), not an implementation detail (\"calls validateEmail\")\n2. **Real assertions?** Assertions check outputs/effects, not mock internals\n3. **Edge cases?** Empty, null, boundary, error, and concurrent cases covered where relevant\n4. **No tautologies?** Tests don't just restate what the code obviously does\n5. **Exploit classes covered?** Every tested function has the six rule-6a assertions (two fixture values per asserted field, a near-miss negative, a multi-element and an empty input, an accept and a reject case of the same shape, a surfaced error, a dependency assertion); a missing one is a check-5 failure and names the class.\n\nIf any check fails, dispatch Tess again with specific feedback about what's weak. Render the retry the same way as the initial dispatch — never author it by hand:\n\n```bash\npython3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/work/scripts/render_prompt.py ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/work/references/tess-retry-prompt.md \\\n  --out docs/dev/tmp/dispatch-tess-<task-id>-retry-<n>.txt \\\n  --set-file QUALITY_FEEDBACK=docs/dev/tmp/tess-<task-id>-gate-<n>.txt \\\n  --set-file TASK_DESCRIPTION=<the same scratch file step 2.7 used> \\\n  --set-file TASK_ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA=docs/dev/tmp/tess-<task-id>-acceptance.txt --dispatch-kind tess --dispatch-task <task-id>\n```\n\nWrite the gate findings (one per line) to the `QUALITY_FEEDBACK` scratch file with the Write tool. Max 2 quality gate retries.\n\n### Style limits on the test files\n\nOnce the four checks pass, run the step-5.65 gate over the test files only, so an oversized test file is split by a Tess retry now (cheap, tests-only) instead of an Ivan style-fix dispatch after implementation (PRD 00197; `tess-prompt.md` rule 12 already tells her the limits). Build the diff the way `references/style-gate.md` § Diff construction does, over this task's test files alone: `git diff <task_base_sha> --output=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/test-diff-<task-id>.txt -- <tracked test files>` (`--output=` goes before `--`; after it git reads it as a pathspec and prints the diff to stdout instead), then `git diff --no-index -- /dev/null <file> >> ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/test-diff-<task-id>.txt` per untracked test file (exit 1 is the normal case; only exit ≥2 is a failure), then `mv ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/test-diff-<task-id>.txt docs/dev/tmp/test-diff-<task-id>.txt`. Then:\n\n```bash\npython3 ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/work/scripts/check_style_limits.py --diff docs/dev/tmp/test-diff-<task-id>.txt <each new or changed test file as an absolute path>\n```\n\nExit 0 is clean. Exit 1 is a quality-gate failure: write the violation lines to the `QUALITY_FEEDBACK` scratch file and render the retry above; it counts toward the two quality-gate retries. Exit 2 (the script could not run) is not a Tess failure: name it in the task output and proceed, step 5.65 measures the same files again after implementation.\n\n## Retry Prompt (after quality gate failure)\n\nThe retry template lives in `work/references/tess-retry-prompt.md` (single\nsource of truth, registry-shaped single-brace placeholders — `render_prompt.py`\nconsumes it directly; see `agent-registry.md` for the placeholder table). It\ntakes `{QUALITY_FEEDBACK}`, `{TASK_DESCRIPTION}` and\n`{TASK_ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA}`, and bakes in the same fixed guards the initial\ntemplate carries (read-only scope, 100K abort line, dispatch prologue,\nASSUMPTIONS footer).\n\n`{QUALITY_FEEDBACK}` is the step-2.8 gate's own findings, one per line, e.g.:\n\n- Test \"handles validation\" is too vague. Name the specific behavior.\n- Test 3 would pass with a function that always returns true. Add constraints.\n- No edge case for empty input.\n\n---\ncatchup: skip\ndesign: run\ndefault_model: opus\nmodel_tier_rationale: concurrency, cancellation and subprocess lifecycle decide correctness\n---\n\n# backup: bounded parallel jobs with clean cancellation\n\nSource: `docs/dev/project-management/intake/processed/00107-backup-multi-source-parallel/discovery.md` (Q7, Q10, Q11). Depends on PRD 00110 (duplicate-output rejection, run-wide `now`). PRD 00112 depends on this one.\n\n## Overview\n\n### Problem Statement\n`Runner.run` loops over instances one after another (`src/tools/backup/runner.py:31`). A run of many large trees takes the sum of all archive times. The system-tar writer blocks in `subprocess.run` (`src/tools/backup/capabilities/tar_archive.py:340`), so a Ctrl-C cannot stop the child cleanly, and nothing cancels a writer that is mid-archive.\n\n### Target Users\nThe solo operator who runs many instances unattended and sometimes interrupts a run.\n\n### Success Metrics\n- `uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_parallel_runner.py -q` passes.\n- `test_never_exceeds_jobs` proves at most `jobs` writers are active at once with five ready instances and default `jobs=3`.\n- `test_cancel_cleans_temp_files_both_engines` passes: after cancellation no `*.tmp` sibling and no new final archive remain, and a prior output is unchanged.\n\n## Functional Decomposition\n\n### Capability: Bounded concurrency\nRun up to N instances at once.\n\n#### Feature: `--jobs`\n- **Description**: CLI option and `Runner` parameter for the worker count.\n- **Inputs**: `--jobs N` (a plain `int` option, default 3; the callback rejects a value below 1 with `console.failure(\"--jobs must be at least 1\")` followed by `raise SystemExit(1)`, never a Click parse-time validator, per the AGENTS.md Click validation rule); `Runner(cfg, dry_run=..., jobs=N)`.\n- **Outputs**: instances run on a `concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=jobs)` in `(order, name)` submit order; `--jobs 1` is sequential.\n- **Behavior**: order is launch priority only, not a completion barrier. Results are yielded to the caller in completion order, from the calling thread only; workers never write to the console. A worker exception becomes a failed `StepResult` for that instance and does not stop the others. Instances rejected by PRD 00110's duplicate check are never submitted.\n\n### Capability: Cancellation\nStop everything cleanly on interrupt.\n\n#### Feature: Cooperative cancel signal\n- **Description**: A `threading.Event` named `cancel` is passed to every capability call as the kwarg `cancel`.\n- **Inputs**: `cancel: threading.Event`.\n- **Outputs**: raising `ArchiveCancelled` (new exception in `src/tools/backup/capabilities/tar_archive.py`) from the Python writer, checked before each member is added, after which the existing `except BaseException` cleanup removes the temp file.\n- **Behavior**: the system-tar writer starts tar with `subprocess.Popen` and waits with a loop of `process.communicate(timeout=0.2)` retries, passing `input=` on the first call only (a later call with `input` raises `ValueError`), checking `cancel` between retries; on cancel it calls `process.terminate()`, then `process.kill()` if it has not exited after 5 seconds, waits, removes the temp file, and raises `ArchiveCancelled`. `ArchiveCancelled` is not turned into a fallback to the Python engine.\n\n#### Feature: Interrupt handling\n- **Description**: Ctrl-C in the CLI stops the run.\n- **Inputs**: `KeyboardInterrupt` in the calling thread while waiting for results.\n- **Outputs**: `cancel` set; no new instance starts (`executor.shutdown(wait=True, cancel_futures=True)`); every still-active writer cleans up; completed archives and prior outputs are untouched; the CLI prints `console.failure(\"interrupted: ...\")` naming the instances that did not finish, and exits 130 via `raise SystemExit(130)`, the pattern `src/tools/backup/cli.py` already uses for its nonzero exits.\n- **Behavior**: an interrupted instance yields `StepResult(name, success=False, message=\"cancelled\")`. `Runner.run` re-raises nothing; it ends after yielding the cancelled results and exposes `Runner.interrupted: bool` so the CLI can pick exit 130.\n\n## Structural Decomposition\n\n### Repository Structure\n\n```\nsrc/tools/backup/capabilities/tar_archive.py   # Maps to: Cooperative cancel signal\nsrc/tools/backup/runner.py                     # Maps to: --jobs, Interrupt handling\nsrc/tools/backup/cli.py                        # Maps to: --jobs, Interrupt handling\ntests/tools/backup/test_parallel_runner.py     # Maps to: all features\ntests/tools/backup/test_invariants.py          # Maps to: invariant guard (read only unless it fails)\ndocs/source/tools/backup.rst                   # Maps to: documentation\nCHANGELOG.md                                   # Maps to: documentation\n```\n\n### Module: tar_archive\n- **Maps to capability**: Cancellation\n- **Responsibility**: observe `cancel`, stop both engines cleanly.\n- **Location**: `src/tools/backup/capabilities/tar_archive.py`\n- **Exports**: `ArchiveCancelled`\n- New symbols: `ArchiveCancelled`, `cancel`, `jobs`, `interrupted`, `test_never_exceeds_jobs`, `test_cancel_cleans_temp_files_both_engines`\n\n### Module: runner\n- **Maps to capability**: Bounded concurrency, Cancellation\n- **Responsibility**: pool, completion-order results, cancel on interrupt.\n- **Location**: `src/tools/backup/runner.py`\n- **Exports**: `Runner(cfg, dry_run=False, jobs=3)`, `Runner.interrupted`.\n\n### Module: cli\n- **Maps to capability**: Bounded concurrency, Cancellation\n- **Responsibility**: `--jobs`, exit 130 on interrupt.\n- **Location**: `src/tools/backup/cli.py`\n\n## Dependency Graph\n\n### Foundation Layer (Phase 0)\n- **tar_archive**: no dependencies; built first.\n\n### Core Layer (Phase 1)\n- **runner**: Depends on [tar_archive]\n\n### Integration Layer (Phase 2)\n- **cli**: Depends on [runner]\n\n## Implementation Phases\n\n### Phase 0: Foundation\n**Goal**: Both writers can be cancelled.\n\n**Tasks**:\n- [ ] Add `ArchiveCancelled`, the `cancel` kwarg, the per-member check in `_write_archive`, and the Popen/poll/terminate/kill loop in `_write_archive_system_tar` (no deps) - Acceptance: `uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_parallel_runner.py -k \"cancel_cleans or terminates_tar\" -q` passes; the system-tar test uses a stub `tar` script on `PATH` that sleeps.\n\n**Exit Criteria**: setting the event during a write leaves no temp file and no new final archive.\n\n### Phase 1: Core\n**Goal**: The runner runs bounded and cancels.\n\n**Tasks**:\n- [ ] Replace the sequential loop in `src/tools/backup/runner.py` by the pool described above, with `jobs`, completion-order yielding, `cancel`, and `interrupted` (depends on: Phase 0) - Acceptance: `uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_parallel_runner.py -k \"jobs or order or failure_isolated or interrupt\" -q` passes.\n\n**Exit Criteria**: five instances with `jobs=3` never run more than three at once; one failing instance does not stop the others.\n\n### Phase 2: Integration\n**Goal**: CLI exposes it and it is documented.\n\n**Tasks**:\n- [ ] Add `--jobs` and the exit-130 path to `src/tools/backup/cli.py` (depends on: Phase 1) - Acceptance: `uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_parallel_runner.py -k cli -q` passes, including `--jobs 0` exiting 1 with `--jobs must be at least 1` in the console output.\n- [ ] Document `--jobs`, ordering and Ctrl-C behaviour in `docs/source/tools/backup.rst`; add an `Added` line under `[Unreleased]` in `CHANGELOG.md` (depends on: Phase 1) - Acceptance: `rg -c \"\\-\\-jobs\" docs/source/tools/backup.rst CHANGELOG.md` prints a count of at least 1 for each file.\n\n**Exit Criteria**: `uv run pytest tests/tools/backup -q` passes (including `tests/tools/backup/test_invariants.py`: no `print(`, `sys.exit`, `console.panic` in library files); `uv run ruff check src/tools/backup tests/tools/backup` and `uv run mypy src/tools/backup` are clean.\n\n## Test Strategy\n\n### Critical Scenarios\n- **Happy path**: five instances, `jobs=3` (instrumented fake capability counting concurrency) → Expected: peak concurrency 3, all five results, launch order by `(order, name)`.\n- **Edge case**: `jobs=1` → Expected: strictly sequential in `(order, name)` order.\n- **Edge case**: a later instance finishes before an earlier one → Expected: it is yielded first.\n- **Error case**: one instance raises → Expected: failed step for it, the rest succeed, overall exit 1.\n- **Error case**: interrupt with two active and two queued → Expected: queued never start, active ones clean up, completed results kept, exit 130.\n- **Error case**: interrupt during a system-tar archive that would replace an existing output → Expected: previous output bytes unchanged.\n\n## Risks\n\n- **Disk contention**: three writers on one disk may be slower. Mitigation: `--jobs 1`; no speed-up is promised.\n- **Orphan child or temp file**: a killed Python or tar process leaves a temp file. Mitigation: terminate then kill with a bounded wait; temp cleanup in `finally`; tested for both engines.\n- **Thread-safety of shared state**: workers must not mutate shared objects. Mitigation: each call gets its own kwargs; only the calling thread renders.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
